ntdll: the unix EC bitmap query is bounded too.

0041 bounded `RtlIsEcCode` and deliberately left its unix twin `is_ec_code`
(`dlls/ntdll/unix/unix_private.h:475`) alone, on two grounds it stated plainly:
nothing had been observed faulting there, and it is an inline on a hot path the
change had not been measured against. `docs/TODO.md` has carried that as an open
item ever since, closed by *"either it is bounded the same way, or a measurement
shows the bound costs more than the fault it prevents"*.

**No measurement is needed, because the second ground is false.** `is_ec_code`
has exactly one caller in the whole tree -- `signal_set_full_context`
(`dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_arm64.c:366`) -- and that function has exactly two:
`NtContinueEx` (`dlls/ntdll/unix/server.c:875`) and `signal_start_thread`
(`signal_arm64.c:1558`). That is one call per `NtContinue` and one per thread
start. It is syscall granularity, order thousands per session on the evidence in
`docs/TODO.md` (#50's 34 exception deliveries, #54's 1,500-line probe loop), and
it is nothing per frame and nothing per branch target. **The hot path 0041 was
protecting is the other copy**: FEX's per-branch-target
`BranchTargetInMultiblockRange` calls the exported `RtlIsEcCode`, which already
pays this comparison.

So the bound costs one compare and one predictable branch on a path that has
just made a syscall. There is no version of the trade in which that loses to a
wild read.

**And the hazard is reachable, not theoretical.** The value tested is
`frame->pc` as `NtSetContextThread` has just written it out of a `CONTEXT` the
caller supplied, so an `NtContinue` with a `Pc` above the address space indexes
`Peb->EcCodeBitMap` at up to `ptr >> 15` bytes past the mapping. That is the
same shape as the fault 0041 fixed, in a query whose honest answer is `FALSE`.

**One correction to the record while it is being closed.** The item was written
down as an unbounded *search*. There is no search: the function is a single
indexed bitmap load, `page_size` is a compile-time constant
(`unix_private.h:153`) so the divide is a shift, and what is unbounded is the
index. Nobody should go looking for a loop to cap.

The bound is `arm64ec_map_pages`, set from the size `alloc_arm64ec_map` actually
handed to `map_view` rather than recomputed from `address_space_limit`. That
distinction is the expensive half of 0041 -- its first version derived a bound
from `NtQueryVirtualMemory`, got one region instead of the whole reservation,
and made real EC addresses report as not-EC, which killed Metro 2033. A bound
taken from the allocation cannot come out short. The long form of all of this is
in the comment the patch adds, where the next reader will be.

Not for upstream: the fault it prevents is reachable here because FEX drives
ARM64EC contexts, and the argument above is about this stack.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/unix_private.h b/dlls/ntdll/unix/unix_private.h
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/unix_private.h
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/unix_private.h
@@ -472,10 +472,69 @@
             (char *)sp <= (char *)get_syscall_frame());
 }
 
+extern ULONG_PTR arm64ec_map_pages;
+
+/* Vessel: the unix twin of RtlIsEcCode, bounded for the same reason and by a
+ * different quantity.
+ *
+ * patches/wine/0041 bounded the PE-side RtlIsEcCode after an unbounded index into
+ * Peb->EcCodeBitMap raised an access violation inside a query whose honest answer
+ * is FALSE -- 34 deliveries of one fault at one address, 7200 bytes of guest stack
+ * each, until the stack was gone. That patch deliberately left this copy alone,
+ * and said why: "identical shape, identical hazard, but nothing has been observed
+ * faulting there and it is an inline on a hot path this change has not been
+ * measured against."
+ *
+ * **The second half of that reason was wrong, and correcting it is what makes the
+ * bound obviously affordable rather than a thing to measure.** This inline is not
+ * on a hot path. It has exactly one caller -- signal_set_full_context
+ * (unix/signal_arm64.c:366), which asks whether the pc it is about to resume is EC
+ * code and redirects the thread to KiUserEmulationDispatcher when it is not -- and
+ * that function is reached from exactly two places: NtContinueEx
+ * (unix/server.c:875) and signal_start_thread (unix/signal_arm64.c:1558). So this
+ * runs once per NtContinue and once per thread start: syscall granularity, order
+ * thousands per session, and nothing per frame or per branch target. The
+ * per-branch-target query FEX makes from BranchTargetInMultiblockRange goes to the
+ * exported RtlIsEcCode, which is the copy 0041 already pays a comparison on. The
+ * hot path that note was protecting does not run through here.
+ *
+ * What the bound costs is therefore one compare and one predictable branch on a
+ * path that has just performed a syscall (NtSetContextThread) and may have taken
+ * pthread_sigmask. What it prevents is a read up to (ptr >> 15) bytes past the end
+ * of the map, from a value the guest owns: the pc tested is frame->pc as
+ * NtSetContextThread has just written it out of a caller-supplied CONTEXT, so an
+ * NtContinue with a wild Pc is enough. There is no measurement in which the first
+ * outweighs the second, which is the "Done when" this closes.
+ *
+ * **Note also what this is not: there is no search here to bound.** The function
+ * is a single indexed load -- page_size is a compile-time constant
+ * (unix_private.h:153), so ptr / page_size is a shift -- and it was recorded as
+ * "unbounded" meaning the *index* is unbounded, not that anything scans. A reader
+ * looking for a loop to cap will not find one, and capping an iteration count is
+ * not the fix.
+ *
+ * **The bound is the map's own extent, and that choice is the part of 0041 that
+ * cost a build.** Its first version derived the limit from NtQueryVirtualMemory,
+ * which answers about one region rather than the whole reservation; the bound came
+ * out short, real EC addresses began reporting as not-EC, and Metro 2033 went from
+ * working to dying in an unrepairable write fault. A wrong FALSE is a bigger lie
+ * than the read it prevents -- it tells the emulator that native ARM64EC code is
+ * x86-64 to translate. arm64ec_map_pages is not a query about the allocation: it
+ * is the size alloc_arm64ec_map() passed to map_view (unix/virtual.c:3052) times
+ * eight, so it is exact by construction and cannot come out short. map_view may
+ * round the mapping further up and never down, and the pages between the two lie
+ * above address_space_limit, where nothing can be code anyway.
+ *
+ * The map check is separate from the page check on purpose: a process that has
+ * loaded no EC image has no map at all, and NULL with a zero bound would otherwise
+ * dereference before the comparison could save it. */
 static inline BOOL is_ec_code( ULONG_PTR ptr )
 {
     const UINT64 *map = (const UINT64 *)peb->EcCodeBitMap;
     ULONG_PTR page = ptr / page_size;
+
+    if (!map) return FALSE;
+    if (page >= arm64ec_map_pages) return FALSE;
     return (map[page / 64] >> (page & 63)) & 1;
 }
 
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -329,6 +329,13 @@
 static void *host_addr_space_limit;  /* top of the host virtual address space */
 
 static struct file_view *arm64ec_view;
+/* Vessel: how many pages arm64ec_view covers, which is the bound for is_ec_code().
+ * Taken from the size actually handed to map_view rather than recomputed from
+ * address_space_limit, so it cannot disagree with the allocation. The comment on
+ * is_ec_code() in unix_private.h says why a bound that comes out short is worse
+ * than no bound at all. Zero until alloc_arm64ec_map() runs, which is correct:
+ * with no map, no address is EC code. */
+ULONG_PTR arm64ec_map_pages = 0;
 static const ptrdiff_t max_try_map_step = 0x40000000;
 static BOOL increase_try_map_step = TRUE;
 
@@ -3059,6 +3066,7 @@
         exit(1);
     }
     peb->EcCodeBitMap = arm64ec_view->base;
+    arm64ec_map_pages = size * 8;  /* Vessel: one bit per page, so eight pages per byte */
 }
 
 
